{
  "requirement": "The SDK presents node inputs using the returned value's `NodeContents::format`\nunder {cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.format`.\nIts text and fingerprint describe the same retained observation.\n\n```rust,ignore\nlet contents = domain.node_content(&node_id)?;\nlet text = contents.format();\n```\n\nLoading and fallible preparation occur in `Domain::node_content` under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content`. For inputs queued on an ask\nbuilder, these failures return `ValidationError::Graph`. During a `read_node`\ntool call, they are returned to the agent as a tool error, so it can continue\nand recover.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately cover `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-calls.judge.rendering`.\n\n**Obligations and the tests that cover them**\n\n1. **Inputs are presented through the returned value's `NodeContents::format`.**\n   - For inputs queued on an ask builder, `default_tool_accepts_scalar_results_and_keeps_the_exact_prefix` installs a custom formatter. It asserts that the first user message contains `formatted input: source`. This shows the output of `format` is what the agent sees. The raw content string is not.\n   - `explicit_insertions_preserve_order_repetition_and_selection_snapshots` checks that several queued inputs are rendered in order.\n   - For the `read_node` tool path, `validator_agent_executions_isolate_conversations_and_dependency_traces` uses the `distinct_pages` formatter. Its `ParallelConversations` provider asserts that each conversation's history contains its own formatted contents. It also asserts that the last delivered page contains them.\n   - `domain_formats_supplied_contents_without_resolving_a_replacement` in `src/sdk.rs` checks the habeas domain's `format` output: it contains the supplied text and the path.\n\n2. **Text and fingerprint describe the same retained observation.**\n   - `identities_remain_stable_and_fingerprinting_uses_the_supplied_snapshot` reads a snapshot, then changes the underlying contents.\n   - It asserts that `format()` still returns \"before\". It asserts that `fingerprint()` still equals the snapshot fingerprint. It asserts that the domain was read only once.\n   - So text and fingerprint come from the same retained read, with no re-read.\n   - `graph_read_pages_retain_the_prepared_snapshot_after_contents_change` is not marked for this requirement. It supports the same point.\n\n3. **Loading and preparation failures on ask-builder inputs return `ValidationError::Graph`.**\n   - `queued_reads_selection_and_rendering_fail_before_agent_invocation` sets `fail_read`. It asserts `Err(ValidationError::Graph(\"read\"))`.\n   - It then sets `fail_prepare`. It asserts `Err(ValidationError::Graph(\"render\"))`.\n   - It also asserts that no dependencies are recorded and that no agent request is made.\n\n4. **During a `read_node` call, failures go to the agent as a tool error so it can recover.**\n   - `node_preparation_tool_errors_allow_recovery_without_recording_undelivered_contents` makes preparation fail.\n   - It asserts that the next request carries a single `read_node` tool result with `is_error` set and the error text.\n   - It asserts that the conversation continues to a successful `submit_result`. It asserts that no dependency is recorded.\n\n**Remaining observation**\n\n- A loading failure during a `read_node` call is not tested separately from a preparation failure.\n- `Domain::node_content` returns one error type for both. The requirement does not distinguish the two paths for tool calls.\n- Preparation failure exercises the same tool-error path, so I do not count this as a gap.\n\n**Other requirements**\n\n- Loading and preparation (`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content`) and the `format` contract (`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.format`) are cited context. Their own tests are not reassessed here.\n- The reviewed tests still exercise the \"no further reads\" property of `format`, in `identities_remain_stable_and_fingerprinting_uses_the_supplied_snapshot`.\n\nNo gaps were found, and there are no `{fixme}` acknowledgments.",
